#====================================================================
# Draft knowledge entry - test-framework self-test suites contain
# deliberately-failing negative-path cases: run exit code 1 is the
# EXPECTED product, and the recipe build() upstream-test gate must
# mirror the upstream harness semantics (compile = hard gate, run =
# framework report required, rc ignored)
# Source package: cheat/1.0.4 (tests/*.c, makefile `test` target)
#====================================================================

id:                     DRAFT-cheat-1.0.4-framework-self-test-negative-path
trigger_tool:           clang|cc|make|ctest|conan
phase:                  build|test
error_signature:        1 failed of [0-9]+ run|FAILURE$|assertion in 'failure' failed|negative path

severity:               2
category:               testing|test-framework|upstream-tests|gating
match_keywords:         unit testing framework, self-test, negative path, deliberately failing test, crash test, signal, assertion false, exit code 1, make test, upstream harness, ignore exit code, FAILURE report, recipe build(), test gate
title:                  Test-framework self-test suites deliberately include negative-path cases that exit non-zero: the recipe build() upstream-test gate must follow the upstream harness semantics (compile failures are the hard gate; a run is fine if the framework printed its report, whatever the exit code)

root_cause:             A unit-testing framework's own test suite MUST
                        contain cases that fail by design (assertion
                        deliberately false, crash/signal injection, output
                        over-limit, random asserts) to prove the framework
                        can DETECT and REPORT failures. Consequently many
                        of its test binaries exit rc=1 with a FAILURE
                        report — that is the expected product, not a
                        defect. Upstream harnesses encode this: cheat's
                        makefile uses `- ./example -s` (make: ignore the
                        failing recipe line) and its `test` script never
                        checks `$?`. A recipe build() that treats any
                        non-zero test exit as a gate failure will therefore
                        fail on a fully healthy upstream suite.
affected_scope:         Recipes for header-only or embedded test frameworks
                        (catch2/doctest/cmocka/unity/cheat and similar)
                        that integrate the upstream tests into conan
                        build() as the upstream-test gate.

verification_cmd:       cd <src>; for i in tests/*.c; do cc -I . -o /tmp/t -w "$i" || exit 1; /tmp/t -s; done; echo "all files compiled and produced a framework report"

rec_priority:           1
rec_approach:           harness-semantics-test-gate
rec_description:        In the recipe build(), compile every upstream test
                        file (compile failure = real platform defect =
                        hard gate), then RUN each binary and require only
                        that it produced the framework's report output
                        (SUCCESS / FAILURE / "N ... of K run"). Treat
                        rc<=127 with a report as executed-and-fine; flag
                        only rc>=128 (crash outside the framework) or
                        missing report as a run failure. Record the
                        per-case pass/fail counts in the ledger with the
                        negative-path cases explained as upstream
                        by-design tests.
rec_command:            clang -D_POSIX_C_SOURCE=200112L -I . -o <prog> -w tests/<name>.c && <prog> -s
rec_auto_fix:           false
rec_impl:               recipe-build

fallback_priority:      2
fallback_approach:      run-upstream-make-test
fallback_description:   If a project ships a `make test` target, invoke it
                        exactly as upstream does (it already ignores the
                        failing recipe lines where intended) and gate only
                        on the target's own behavior.
fallback_command:       make test
fallback_auto_fix:      false
fallback_impl:          build-option

forbid_1_pattern:       raise.*ConanException.*run.*failed|exit.*!=.*0.*test.*fail
forbid_1_reason:        Treating every non-zero test exit as failure
                        misclassifies by-design negative-path cases and
                        makes healthy frameworks fail their own gate.
forbid_1_consequence:   Healthy upstream suites never pass; the recipe
                        cannot be merged even though the framework is
                        fully functional on OHOS.

forbid_2_pattern:       skip.*upstream.*test|BUILD_TESTING=OFF.*justify
forbid_2_reason:        Skipping the upstream suite to dodge negative-path
                        cases hides the real compilation verification the
                        gate exists to provide.
forbid_2_consequence:   Compile-level regressions go undetected; the gate
                        becomes meaningless.

principles:             P-TEST-001, P-BUILD-002
causes:
caused_by:
similar_to:             [E370, DRAFT-box2d-2.4.1-ctest-empty-suite-false-pass]
conflicts_with:
prerequisite_of:

doc_file:               knowledge/drafts/DRAFT-cheat-1.0.4-framework-self-test-negative-path.md
last_updated:           2026-09-19
source_event:           cheat/1.0.4 adaptation: 25/25 upstream test files
                        compiled and ran; 26 framework-level FAILURE cases
                        are all by-design negative-path tests; recipe gate
                        counts executed-with-report, not rc==0.
authors:                junshi_liu
source_pr:
source_commit:

metrics_success_count:  1
metrics_fail_count:     0
metrics_confidence:     0.8
metrics_last_used:      2026-09-19

draft:                  true
contributor_id:         junshi_liu
